DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The SSDDPP__GGEETT88(), SSDDPP__GGEETT1166(), SSDDPP__GGEETT3322(), SSDDPP__GGEETT6644() and SSDDPP__GGEETT112288()
     macros are used to get byte, short, long, long long and 128-bit integer
     from the buffer pointed by _c_p pointer.  The pointer is automatically ad‐
     vanced.

     The SSDDPP__PPUUTT88(), SSDDPP__PPUUTT1166(), SSDDPP__PPUUTT3322(), SSDDPP__PPUUTT6644() and SSDDPP__PPUUTT112288()
     macros are used to put byte, short, long, long long and 128-bit integer
     into the buffer pointed by _c_p pointer.  The pointer is automatically ad‐
     vanced.

     SSDDPP__GGEETT__UUUUIIDD112288() and SSDDPP__PPUUTT__UUUUIIDD112288() macros are used to get and put
     128-bit UUID into the buffer pointed by _c_p pointer.  The pointer is auto‐
     matically advanced.

     The ssddpp__ooppeenn() and ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall() functions each return a pointer to an
     opaque object describing SDP session.  The _l argument passed to
     ssddpp__ooppeenn() function should point to a source BD_ADDR.  If source BD_ADDR
     is NULL then source address NG_HCI_BDADDR_ANY is used.  The _r argument
     passed to ssddpp__ooppeenn() function should point to a non-NULL remote BD_ADDR.
     Remote BD_ADDR cannot be NG_HCI_BDADDR_ANY.  The ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall() func‐
     tion takes path to the control socket and opens a connection to a local
     SDP server.  If path to the control socket is NULL then default
     _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_s_d_p path will be used.

     The ssddpp__cclloossee() function terminates active SDP session and deletes SDP
     session object.  The _x_s parameter should point to a valid SDP session ob‐
     ject created with ssddpp__ooppeenn() or ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall().

     The ssddpp__eerrrroorr() function returns last error that is stored inside SDP
     session object.  The _x_s parameter should point to a valid SDP session ob‐
     ject created with ssddpp__ooppeenn() or ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall().  The error value re‐
     turned can be converted to a human readable message by calling
     strerror(3) function.

     The ssddpp__ggeett__llccaaddddrr() function returns the SDP session actual source
     BD_ADDR.  The _x_s parameter should point to a valid SDP session object
     created with ssddpp__ooppeenn().  The _l parameter should point to a buffer in
     which the value for the requested BD_ADDR is to be returned.
     ssddpp__ggeett__llccaaddddrr() function is useful if the current SDP session has been
     opened with the NG_HCI_BDADDR_ANY value passed as a source address.

     The ssddpp__sseeaarrcchh() function is used to perform SDP Service Search Attribute
     Request.  The _x_s parameter should point to a valid SDP session object
     created with ssddpp__ooppeenn() or ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall().  The _p_p parameter is a Ser‐
     vice Search Pattern - an array of one or more Service Class IDs.  The
     maximum number of Service Class IDs in the array is 12.  The _p_l_e_n parame‐
     ter is the length of the Service Search pattern.  The _a_p parameter is an
     Attribute ID Range List - an array of one or more SDP Attribute ID Range.
     Each attribute ID Range is encoded as a 32-bit unsigned integer data ele‐
     ment, where the high order 16 bits are interpreted as the beginning at‐
     tribute ID of the range and the low order 16 bits are interpreted as the
     ending attribute ID of the range.  The attribute IDs contained in the At‐
     tribute ID Ranges List must be listed in ascending order without duplica‐
     tion of any attribute ID values.  Note that all attributes may be re‐
     quested by specifying a range of 0x0000-0xFFFF.  The _a_l_e_n parameter is
     the length of the Attribute ID Ranges List.  The SSDDPP__AATTTTRR__RRAANNGGEE(_l_o, _h_i)
     macro can be used to prepare Attribute ID Range.  The _v_p parameter should
     be an array of _s_d_p___a_t_t_r___t structures.  Each _s_d_p___a_t_t_r___t structure de‐
     scribes single SDP attribute and defined as follows:

           struct sdp_attr {
                   uint16_t        flags;
           #define SDP_ATTR_OK             (0 << 0)
           #define SDP_ATTR_INVALID        (1 << 0)
           #define SDP_ATTR_TRUNCATED      (1 << 1)
                   uint16_t        attr;  /* SDP_ATTR_xxx */
                   uint32_t        vlen;  /* length of the value[] in bytes */
                   uint8_t        *value; /* base pointer */
           };
           typedef struct sdp_attr         sdp_attr_t;
           typedef struct sdp_attr *       sdp_attr_p;

     The caller of the ssddpp__sseeaarrcchh() function is expected to prepare the array
     of _s_d_p___a_t_t_r structures and for each element of the array both _v_l_e_n and
     _v_a_l_u_e must be set.  The ssddpp__sseeaarrcchh() function will fill each _s_d_p___a_t_t_r___t
     structure with attribute and value, i.e., it will set _f_l_a_g_s, _a_t_t_r and
     _v_l_e_n fields.  The actual value of the attribute will be copied into _v_a_l_u_e
     buffer.  Note: attributes are returned in the order they appear in the
     Service Search Attribute Response.  SDP server could return several at‐
     tributes for the same record.  In this case the order of the attributes
     will be: all attributes for the first records, then all attributes for
     the second record etc.

     The ssddpp__aattttrr22ddeesscc() and ssddpp__uuuuiidd22ddeesscc() functions each take a numeric at‐
     tribute ID/UUID value and convert it to a human readable description.

     The ssddpp__rreeggiisstteerr__sseerrvviiccee() function is used to register service with the
     local SDP server.  The _x_s_s parameter should point to a valid SDP session
     object obtained from ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall().  The _u_u_i_d parameter is a SDP Ser‐
     vice Class ID for the service to be registered.  The _b_d_a_d_d_r parameter
     should point to a valid BD_ADDR.  The service will be only advertised if
     request was received by the local device with _b_d_a_d_d_r.  If _b_d_a_d_d_r is set
     to NG_HCI_BDADDR_ANY then the service will be advertised to any remote
     devices that queries for it.  The _d_a_t_a and _d_a_t_a_l_e_n parameters specify
     data and size of the data for the service.  Upon successful return
     ssddpp__rreeggiisstteerr__sseerrvviiccee() will populate _h_a_n_d_l_e with the SDP record handle.
     This parameter is optional and can be set to NULL.

     The ssddpp__uunnrreeggiisstteerr__sseerrvviiccee() function is used to unregister service with
     the local SDP server.  The _x_s_s parameter should point to a valid SDP ses‐
     sion object obtained from ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall().  The _h_a_n_d_l_e parameter should
     contain a valid SDP record handle of the service to be unregistered.

     The ssddpp__cchhaannggee__sseerrvviiccee() function is used to change data associated with
     the existing service on the local SDP server.  The _x_s_s parameter should
     point to a valid SDP session object obtained from ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall().  The
     _h_a_n_d_l_e parameter should contain a valid SDP record handle of the service
     to be changed.  The _d_a_t_a and _d_a_t_a_l_e_n parameters specify data and size of
     the data for the service.

CCAAVVEEAATT
     When registering services with the local SDP server the application must
     keep the SDP session open.  If SDP session is closed then the local SDP
     server will remove all services that were registered over the session.
     The application is allowed to change or unregister service if it was reg‐
     istered over the same session.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following example shows how to get SDP_ATTR_PROTOCOL_DESCRIPTOR_LIST
     attribute for the SDP_SERVICE_CLASS_SERIAL_PORT service from the remote
     device.

           bdaddr_t       remote;
           uint8_t        buffer[1024];
           void          *ss    = NULL;
           uint16_t       serv  = SDP_SERVICE_CLASS_SERIAL_PORT;
           uint32_t       attr  = SDP_ATTR_RANGE(
                                       SDP_ATTR_PROTOCOL_DESCRIPTOR_LIST,
                                       SDP_ATTR_PROTOCOL_DESCRIPTOR_LIST);
           sdp_attr_t     proto = { SDP_ATTR_INVALID,0,sizeof(buffer),buffer };

           /* Obtain/set remote BDADDR here */

           if ((ss = sdp_open(NG_HCI_BDADDR_ANY, remote)) == NULL)
                   /* exit ENOMEM */
           if (sdp_error(ss) != 0)
                   /* exit sdp_error(ss) */

           if (sdp_search(ss, 1, &serv, 1, &attr, 1, &proto) != 0)
                   /* exit sdp_error(ss) */

           if (proto.flags != SDP_ATTR_OK)
                   /* exit see proto.flags for details */

           /* If we got here then we have attribute value in proto.value */

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     Both ssddpp__ooppeenn() and ssddpp__ooppeenn__llooccaall() will return NULL if memory alloca‐
     tion for the new SDP session object fails.  If the new SDP object was
     created then caller is still expected to call ssddpp__eerrrroorr() to check if
     there was connection error.

     The ssddpp__ggeett__llccaaddddrr(), ssddpp__sseeaarrcchh(), ssddpp__rreeggiisstteerr__sseerrvviiccee(),
     ssddpp__uunnrreeggiisstteerr__sseerrvviiccee(), and ssddpp__cchhaannggee__sseerrvviiccee() functions return non-
     zero value on error.  The caller is expected to call ssddpp__eerrrroorr() to find
     out more about error.

